libzync: Bitcoin Core v28.x (110183746) levers, applied ON TOP of patches 0001, 0002, 0009, 0010.

Every hunk is behind its own #ifdef; a build without the define compiles Core as before. The guest
build defines all of them when HAZYNC_LIBZYNC is set (libzync/src/profile.rs is the one list).
None changes what is checked: each is a cheaper way to compute the same bytes, and every txid, wtxid,
sighash and merkle root depends on them, so a mistake fails a block rather than passing a bad one.

  HAZYNC_SHA_STREAM      crypto/sha256.cpp   CSHA256 state kept in the accelerator's layout
  HAZYNC_D64_STREAM      crypto/sha256.cpp   merkle-node SHA256D64 as 3 accelerator calls, no byte swaps
  HAZYNC_HASHWRITER_BUF  hash.h              HashWriter stages small writes in a 512-byte inline buffer
  HAZYNC_LAZY_TXID       primitives/transaction.{h,cpp}  txid/wtxid computed on first use
  HAZYNC_RAW_TXID        primitives/transaction.h        seed txid/wtxid from the raw bytes just parsed
  HAZYNC_PUBKEY_MEMO     pubkey.cpp          memo of parsed public keys (keyed by the full serialised key)
  HAZYNC_H160_MEMO       script/interpreter.cpp  memo of HASH160 over 33-byte keys (full key compared)
  HAZYNC_LEGACY_SIGHASH  script/interpreter.cpp  legacy SIGHASH_ALL from preserialised slices + midstate
  HAZYNC_AGG_LITE        script/interpreter.cpp  the legacy-sighash cache also keys on the tx generation

Measured motivation for each (block 962,000) is in experimental/libzync-bench/hook/apply_round*.py,
which produced these edits and are kept only as history. Regenerate with scripts/libzync-base.sh.

